 "batch_notes": "Recurring shapes: (1) many general properties gate around a known bug with tc.assume and cite the pin or bug id in a comment (gimli, gitoxide x2, glam, gluon, gltf, good_lp via constraint_coeff snapping) -- this is the main explicit attribution channel here. (2) glam uses the crate's own f64 types as a higher-precision reference; that sits between internal-consistency and model-oracle (I used model-oracle when the f64 value is the sole comparand, internal-consistency when two f32 APIs are the subject). (3) Mathematical inverses (m*m.inverse()==I, transform then inverse, succ/pred, compact of full children) are ambiguous between roundtrip and algebraic-law; I used algebraic-law for arithmetic inverse laws and roundtrip for data conversions/encodings. (4) 'never panics AND <bound>' properties (gimli bounded consumption, h3o mode exclusivity) were classified by the substantive bound per the rubric. (5) half: four general properties are linked directly to bugs and flagged KNOWN FAILURE in the README but are not narrowed, so not tagged pin. (6) gluon/3 and handlebars/1 have an empty test field and no pin; neither is attributable. (7) Quality: READMEs for gimli, glam, glob-less targets (gluon, goblin, h3o, gltf binary tests) say '(no doc comment)' although the tests do carry 'Property:' doc comments, so README extraction misses comments in some layouts (macro-nested or banner-style comments). (8) handlebars' README wording claims 'oracle-independent' for several tests that are ordinary spec postconditions; no taxonomy issue, just terminology."
